<p>Generally, you're not gonna need to mess with the toolkit buttons at the top of the text area all that much, if you're just leaving a normal comment somewhere.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>8) how do I link another article in my comment?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is really the only one of your questions that might come up with some regularity. Linking syntax is explained in <a href="/wiki-syntax">Wiki Syntax</a>, and looks like this: <tt><span style="white-space: pre-wrap;">[URL-name&#32;what&#32;you&#32;want&#32;the&#32;link&#32;to&#32;say]</span></tt>. For example, that link to Wiki Syntax could be written as <tt><span style="white-space: pre-wrap;">[http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/wiki-syntax&#32;Wiki&#32;Syntax]</span></tt>.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Click&nbsp;for&nbsp;answers&nbsp;to&nbsp;your&nbsp;other&nbsp;questions</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>1) what's a heading level 1 (largest)?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What it sounds like, basically: it's a heading for a particular section. You can see them at play on the <a href="https://www.ed.gov/">U.S. Department of Education's website</a> — see how all the lines that say &quot;Press Releases&quot;, &quot;Betsy DeVos&quot;, &quot;Featured&quot;, etc. are all a different font and a larger size? Those are headers in action.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>2) what's a div block element?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Webpages like to section everything on the page into rectangles; you can see that in the general layout of this site pretty clearly, specially the side-bar to your left. divs are those rectangles, and putting one in your comment can let you do some cooler formatting.</p>
<p>For example:</p>
<div style="background-color: salmon;">
<p>The background of this div is the color salmon.</p>
</div>
<p>And:</p>
<div style="border: 6px double black; width: 50%; margin: auto; text-align: center; padding: 0 15px; border-radius: 5px;">
<p>This is a more advanced use of divs.</p>
</div>
<p>This is a pretty basic feature of HTML, and it's not something you really need to worry about all that much for casual site use.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>3) what's a clear floats (both)?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Sometimes, to get more varied web-design, pages &quot;float&quot; divs beside each other, as you can again see on the <a href="https://www.ed.gov/">U.S. Department of Education's website</a>. These don't have borders or background colors, but the &quot;Press Releases&quot; and &quot;Betsy Devos&quot; sections are divs, and they're next to each other. The former is floated left, the later floated right.</p>
<p>Clear floats (both) just means that any part of the page after that isn't going to start floating in the empty space between those two elements; it acts as an invisible barrier that stops floating.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>4) what's a table wizard?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's just Wikidot's little tool for setting up a table, like this:</p>
<table class="wiki-content-table">
<tr>
<th>header</th>
<th>header</th>
<th>header</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>cell-content</td>
<td>cell-content</td>
<td>cell-content</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>cell-content</td>
<td>cell-content</td>
<td>cell-content</td>
</tr>
</table>
<blockquote>
<p>5) what's a table of contents?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's a list of links on a page that lets users quickly navigate between headers; it doesn't work in comments, but you can see it in use <a href="/task-forces">here</a> at the bottom of the &quot;MOBILE TASK FORCES&quot; div.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>6) what's a code block/ code block wizard?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That lets you show off code as plaintext. For instance, here's that more advanced div I showed off earlier:</p>
<div class="code">
<pre>
<code>[[div style=&quot;border: 6px double black; width: 50%; margin: auto; text-align: center; padding: 0 15px; border-radius: 5px;&quot;]]
This is a more advanced use of divs.
[[/div]]</code>
</pre></div>
<p>The code wizard also lets you select what type of programming language the code is, which colors in particular elements for increased readability.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>7) what's a raw text - escape parsing?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's just a way of telling wikidot, &quot;hey, display this as plaintext&quot;. I used this when I was explaining links to you above, so you could see the syntax.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Lastly, I am mostly reaching out for step-by-step tutorials any of you might know and would be willing to share on formatting.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Most of this is just usual Wikidot stuff that's explained in <a href="https://www.wikidot.com/doc:start">their documentation</a>. I self-taught CSS and HTML by writing here, but I know Khan Academy has a <a href="https://www.khanacademy.org/computing/computer-programming/html-css">pretty good, free-to-learn course</a> on the matter. W3Schools also has <a href="https://www.w3schools.com/cssref/">an exhaustive list of CSS properties and explanations thereof</a>, which I reference frequently.</p>
<p>But again, if you're just reading and commenting, a lot of this is just for fun.</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Hopefully that helps!</p>

