<blockquote>
<p>A last question: Is any of this actually necessary in writing SCP articles and tales?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Not particularly! Many popular articles, past and present, use very limited formatting to tell their story; see how <a href="/scp-076">SCP-076</a> and <a href="/scp-5366">SCP-5366</a> both have extremely basic designs. If you understand the basics of what's explained in Wiki Syntax, you'll be fine.</p>
<p>Honestly? The best advice I can give you is just to find a page with formatting you like, and then take that formatting for your own use. I did this all the time when I first started writing and I didn't understand what code meant; eventually it kinda rubs off on you. You can find the page source of a page by scrolling to the bottom, clicking &quot;+ Options&quot;, and then clicking &quot;Page Source&quot;.</p>
<p>For example, if you wanted to use the black-border divs in <a href="/scp-3000">SCP-3000</a>, you could check the page source and find that the code is&#8230;</p>
<div class="code">
<pre>
<code>[[div style=&quot;float: center; border: solid 4px #444444; width: 570px; padding: 1px 15px; margin: 10px 10px 10px 40px; box-shadow: 2px 1px 3px rgba(0,0,0,.2);&quot;]]
text
[[/div]]</code>
</pre></div>
<p>&#8230; and apply that it your article, and even make some changes if you want.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I will also be copying your reply in a word document to use for later, if that is fine with you.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>No worries! Feel free to reach out if you have further tech questions, I'm happy to help.</p>

